 "drivingQuestion": "What do 'go out' and 'be pulled' mean in the mishna?",
 "outcome": "inconclusive",
 "outcomeNote": "Dispute stated; evidence follows in the next unit",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "shabbat.52a.9",
   "daf": "52a",
   "sentenceId": 8,
   "he": "הַסּוּס בְּשֵׁיר וְכוּ׳. מַאי ״יוֹצְאִין״? וּמַאי ״נִמְשָׁכִין״? אָמַר רַב הוּנָא: אוֹ יוֹצְאִין כְּרוּכִין, אוֹ נִמְשָׁכִין. וּשְׁמוּאֵל אָמַר: יוֹצְאִין נִמְשָׁכִין, וְאֵין יוֹצְאִין כְּרוּכִין.",
   "en": "We learned in the mishna: «A horse» may go out «with a chain» around its neck, and so too, all animals that typically have chains around their necks when they go out to the public domain may go out with chains on Shabbat and may be pulled by the chains. The Gemara asks: «What is» the meaning of: «May go out, and what is» the meaning of: «May be pulled? Rav Huna said:» These animals «may go out either» with the chain «wrapped» around their necks as an ornament, «or they may be pulled» by the chain. «And Shmuel said:» These animals «may go out pulled» by the chain; «however, they may not go out» with the chain «wrapped» around their necks as an ornament."
  }
 ],
